> API and interpretation

Word arrays have no implicit byte order or serialization. This primitive
supplies no padding, mode, authentication or key management. Capturing the
actual32-cycle history shares the same canonical encryption/decryption kernel as
the compatibility functions. The original64-flip observations are retained
individually rather than assigned ambiguous histogram bins.

Reusable producers, fixtures and histories live in the canonical C++ library.
Captured records own their values and do not come from a separate report replay.
Selected counters and record/scalar policies are specified at their API
boundaries; they are not peak-byte or wall-time budgets. Synthetic structural
inputs are controlled fixtures for these mechanisms.

> References

- [Wheeler and Needham, TEA, a Tiny Encryption Algorithm, FSE1994,363-366|https://doi.org/10.1007/3-540-60590-8_29]

